llText":"loom data show MARATHON-3"},{"name":"rg","args":[{"type":"word","value":"-n"},{"type":"string","value":"\"Notes|notes|Convention|Workspace|error|slug|UUID|envelope\""},{"type":"word","value":"-i"}],"fullText":"rg -n \"Notes|notes|Convention|Workspace|error|slug|UUID|envelope\" -i"},{"name":"true","args":[],"fullText":"true"},{"name":"loom","args":[{"type":"word","value":"data"},{"type":"word","value":"notes"},{"type":"word","value":"MARATHON-1"}],"fullText":"loom data notes MARATHON-1"},{"name":"head","args":[{"type":"number","value":"-50"}],"fullText":"head -50"},{"name":"loom","args":[{"type":"word","value":"data"},{"type":"word","value":"--help"}],"fullText":"loom data --help"},{"name":"head","args":[{"type":"number","value":"-40"}],"fullText":"head -40"}],"hasRedirects":true,"hasCommandSubstitution":false,"allRedirectsAreDevNull":true,"redirects":[{"operator":">","destinationFds":[2],"targetNodeType":"word","targetText":"/dev/null"},{"operator":">&","destinationFds":[2],"targetNodeType":"number","targetText":"1"},{"operator":">&","destinationFds":[2],"targetNodeType":"number","targetText":"1"},{"operator":">&","destinationFds":[2],"targetNodeType":"number","targetText":"1"},{"operator":">&","destinationFds":[2],"targetNodeType":"number","targetText":"1"}]},"fileOutputThresholdBytes":"40000","isBackground":false,"skipApproval":false,"timeoutBehavior":"TIMEOUT_BEHAVIOR_BACKGROUND","hardTimeout":86400000,"description":"Task notes and loom notes help","closeStdin":true,"conversationId":"4de28d46-57e0-4694-a733-5f0a75259a3e","adminCommandDenylist":[]},"result":{"success":{"command":"loom data show MARATHON-5 --output json | python3 -c 'import json,sys; d=json.loads(sys.stdin.read()[sys.stdin.read().find(\"{\") if False else 0:]); \n' 2>/dev/null; loom data show MARATHON-5 2>&1 | head -100; echo '---NOTES---'; loom data show MARATHON-3 2>&1 | rg -n \"Notes|notes|Convention|Workspace|error|slug|UUID|envelope\" -i || true; loom data notes MARATHON-1 2>&1 | head -50; loom data --help 2>&1 | head -40","workingDirectory":"","exitCode":0,"signal":"","stdout":"time=2026-08-22T22:45:22.943Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:44829\nID:       MARATHON-5\nTitle:    Workspaces: create, list, detail, auto #general\nStatus:   in_progress\nPriority: 0\nType:     task\nAssignee: app-architect-1\nOwner:    marathon-harness\nParent:   MARATHON-1\nLabels:   [architect backend]\n\nDescription:\nWorkspaceObj {id, slug, name, owner_id, join_mode} with join_mode open|invite_only default open. POST /api/workspaces {slug,name} → 201 {workspace, general_channel}; slug kebab ^[a-z0-9-]{2,32}$; duplicate slug 409; creator is owner; auto-create #general. GET /api/workspaces → member/owned list. GET /api/workspaces/{slug} → {workspace, channels, read_state}; omit private channels caller is not in; ?include_archived=1 supported.\n---NOTES---\n12:Implement POST /api/auth/register, POST /api/auth/login, GET /api/auth/me per contract. Username URL-safe slug; password >=8; 201 {user,token} with token length >=16; duplicate username 409; invalid username/short password 400; wrong password 401; missing/invalid bearer 401. No email field. Persist users and tokens cluster-wide so any node accepts the same token.\n19:This task adds the first product API surface for Huddle: `POST /api/auth/register`, `POST /api/auth/login`, and `GET /api/auth/me`, backed by durable users and bearer tokens in the shared SQLite database so any of the three HTTP nodes accepts the same credential. It locks the canonical `UserObj` JSON shape (consumed by Profiles, Workspaces, and the SPA), the error envelope convention for all later REST routes, and a reusable FastAPI auth dependency that every authenticated endpoint will import.\n31:| User / token ids | Stable strings: **UUID4** hex/`str` for `users.id`; tokens identified by hash (no separate public token id) |\n42:- Establishing `UserObj` and the `{ \"error\": { \"code\", \"message\" } }` envelope here prevents Profiles/SPA from inventing competing shapes (MARATHON-2 deferred error envelopes to Auth).\n58:### Conventions inherited from MARATHON-2 (must not diverge)\n63:4. Health stays **unauthenticated** and **unenveloped** (`{\"status\",\"node_id\"}`).\n64:5. Resource ids are stable strings (UUID/ULID family) — this task picks **UUID4 string**.\n86:| Field | Type | Nullability | Notes |\n88:| `id` | string | required | UUID4 string; immutable |\n89:| `username` | string | required | URL-safe slug (see validation); unique cluster-wide; lowercase |\n96:#### Error envelope (all Auth errors; convention for later REST)\n100:  \"error\": {\n101:    \"code\": \"validation_error\",\n109:| `validation_error` | 400 | Bad username, short/long password, malformed JSON body fields, missing required fields |\n113:**Do not** return FastAPI’s default `{\"detail\": ...}` for these routes — normalize to the envelope above so SPA and graders have one shape. (`GET /api/health` remains the documented exception.)\n118:- Meaning: URL-safe slug — lowercase ASCII letters, digits, `_`, `-`; must start with alphanumeric.\n119:- Reject (400 `validation_error`): empty, too short/long, uppercase, spaces, `@` / email-shaped values, leading hyphen/underscore, other punctuation.\n127:- Never echo password in responses or error messages.\n141:| **Client (SPA / grader)** | JSON body `{ \"username\": string, \"password\": string }`. Optional `display_name` allowed; ignore unknown fields or reject — **server must ignore unknown fields** (forward-compatible). While waiting: disable submit / show pending. On 400/409: show `error.message`. On 201: store `token` (SPA: `localStorage` key `huddle.token` — owned by MARATHON-16) and treat `user` as session user. |\n158:**Errors:**\n159:- 400 `validation_error` — invalid username or password length.\n161:- Slow/failure: SQLite busy → retry within connection busy_timeout; if still failing, 503 is acceptable only if implementer adds it; prefer letting the request error after timeout with a logged failure. Client shows generic failure / retry.\n174:**Errors:**\n175:- 400 — missing/invalid body field types (empty username string, etc.) as `validation_error`.\n193:**Errors:**\n203:2. On failure raises/returns **401** with the error envelope.\n220:| Column | Type | Null | Notes |\n222:| `id` | TEXT | NOT NULL | PK, UUID4 string |\n237:| Column | Type | Null | Notes |\n273:| `server/auth/deps.py` | `get_current_user` FastAPI dependency; 401 envelope helper. |\n275:| `server/errors.py` | Small helper to build `JSONResponse` error envelopes (shared). |\n284:| `requirements.txt` | **No new dependencies required** if using stdlib crypto + uuid. Do not add Redis usage for auth. |\n301:1. `server/errors.py` + `db.py` migration v1.\n307:Parallelism: password helpers and error helper can proceed in parallel; routes depend on both + migration.\n315:| Error envelope `{error:{code,message}}` | All subsequent REST tasks + SPA error rendering |\n339:| Workspace/member authz | — | MARATHON-5/6 |\n344:| Changing `/api/health` auth or envelope | `server/app.py` health handler | MARATHON-2 contract |\n359:## Edge Cases & Error Handling\n363:| Duplicate username (UNIQUE race) | Catch integrity error → **409** `conflict`; do not 500 |\n366:| Password length 7 | **400** `validation_error` |\n374:| `Content-Type` not JSON / invalid JSON | **400** (or framework 422 normalized to envelope 400 — implementer must expose envelope, not raw Starlette HTML) |\n390:2. Duplicate username → **409** with error envelope `code=conflict`.\n391:3. Invalid username or password &lt; 8 → **400** with `code=validation_error`.\n398:10. **Negative:** `/api/health` must still return unenveloped `{\"status\":\"ok\",\"node_id\":N}` without auth.\n434:2. Duplicate register shows 409 body with `error.message`.\n440:  [2026-08-22 22:44] marathon-harness: CRITIC REVIEW attempt=1 commit=eeeb8816dac5a08e6f2039f0d4a9635f9ed75229 APPROVED — Auth register/login/me meet design: UserObj (no email), error envelope, scrypt + SHA-256 bearer tokens in SQLite migration v1, get_current_user, cluster shared-DB /me; suite green (33).\nThe 'loom data' subtree contains thin CLI commands that interact\nwith the configured loom issue backend. When --server or LOOM_SERVER_URL is\nset, commands talk to that loom server over HTTP. Without a server, issue\ncommands use the local backend selected by the workspace configuration and\ndaemon IPC environment.\n\nUse 'loom data' commands when:\n  • You want a backend-aware command surface for agents and scripts.\n  • You want to manage agents on a remote loom server.\n  • You are scripting against a hosted loom deployment.\n\nExamples:\n  loom data show <id> --server http://localhost:8080\n  loom data ready --limit 10\n  loom data list --server http://localhost:8080 -o json | jq '.[].id'\n  loom data monitor --server http://localhost:8080\n  loom data agent stop falcon --server http://localhost:8080\n\nRoot-level --server / --workspace are mirrored into LOOM_SERVER_URL /\nLOOM_WORKSPACE in PersistentPreRunE, so 'loom --server URL data show ID'\nand 'loom data show ID --server URL' resolve identically. The data\nsubcommand reads from env, not from its own flag.\n\nUsage:\n  loom data [command]\n\nAvailable Commands:\n  agent       Control a single agent on the loom server (stop/start/restart/yield)\n  agents      List agents managed by the loom server daemon (HTTP)\n  blocked     List issues that are blocked by other open issues (HTTP)\n  claim       Atomically claim an issue\n  close       Close an issue (HTTP)\n  comment     Add a comment to an issue (HTTP)\n  create      Create an issue\n  list        List issues (HTTP)\n  monitor     Show monitor dashboard from a loom server (HTTP, single-shot)\n  ready       List issues that are ready to work on (HTTP)\n  show        Show a single issue by ID (HTTP)\n  update      Update issue fields\n\nFlags:\n  -h, --help            help for data\n  -o, --output string   Output format: text|json (default \"text\")\n      --server string   Loom server base URL (or LOOM_SERVER_URL env var)\n\nGlobal Flags:\n      --backend string      AI backend CLI to use (codex, claude, opencode). Env: LOOM_BACKEND\n      --log-format string   Log format (text|json) (default \"text\")\n      --log-output string   Log output destination (stderr|<filepath>) (default \"stderr\")\n      --workspace string    Workspace ID. Env: LOOM_WORKSPACE\nThe 'loom data' subtree contains thin CLI commands that interact\nwith the configured loom issue backend. When --server or LOOM_SERVER_URL is\nset, commands talk to that loom server over HTTP. Without a server, issue\ncommands use the local backend selected by the workspace configuration and\ndaemon IPC environment.\n\nUse 'loom data' commands when:\n  • You want a backend-aware command surface for agents and scripts.\n  • You want to manage agents on a remote loom server.\n  • You are scripting against a hosted loom deployment.\n\nExamples:\n  loom data show <id> --server http://localhost:8080\n  loom data ready --limit 10\n  loom data list --server http://localhost:8080 -o json | jq '.[].id'\n  loom data monitor --server http://localhost:8080\n  loom data agent stop falcon --server http://localhost:8080\n\nRoot-level --server / --workspace are mirrored into LOOM_SERVER_URL /\nLOOM_WORKSPACE in PersistentPreRunE, so 'loom --server URL data show ID'\nand 'loom data show ID --server URL' resolve identically. The data\nsubcommand reads from env, not from its own flag.\n\nUsage:\n  loom data [command]\n\nAvailable Commands:\n  agent       Control a single agent on the loom server (stop/start/restart/yield)\n  agents      List agents managed by the loom server daemon (HTTP)\n  blocked     List issues that are blocked by other open issues (HTTP)\n  claim       Atomically claim an issue\n  close       Close an issue (HTTP)\n  comment     Add a comment to an issue (HTTP)\n  create      Create an issue\n  