:[],"fullText":"python3"},{"name":"cat","args":[{"type":"word","value":"/work/ws/worktrees/app/app-architect-1/loom-prompts/team-architect.md"}],"fullText":"cat /work/ws/worktrees/app/app-architect-1/loom-prompts/team-architect.md"},{"name":"head","args":[{"type":"number","value":"-100"}],"fullText":"head -100"},{"name":"rg","args":[{"type":"word","value":"-n"},{"type":"word","value":"-i"},{"type":"string","value":"\"dm|dms\""},{"type":"word","value":"/work/ws/worktrees/app/app-architect-1/loom-prompts/"}],"fullText":"rg -n -i \"dm|dms\" /work/ws/worktrees/app/app-architect-1/loom-prompts/"}],"hasRedirects":true,"hasCommandSubstitution":false,"allRedirectsAreDevNull":true,"redirects":[{"operator":"<<","destinationFds":[0],"targetNodeType":"heredoc_redirect","targetText":"python3"},{"operator":"<<","destinationFds":[0],"targetNodeType":"heredoc_redirect","targetText":"python3"}]},"fileOutputThresholdBytes":"40000","isBackground":false,"skipApproval":false,"timeoutBehavior":"TIMEOUT_BEHAVIOR_BACKGROUND","hardTimeout":86400000,"description":"Check migration version conflicts","closeStdin":true,"conversationId":"3f743427-f194-44bc-92dc-836b7b6940f7","adminCommandDenylist":[]},"result":{"failure":{"command":"python3 <<'PY'\nimport json,subprocess,re\nraw=subprocess.check_output(['loom','data','show','MARATHON-8','--output','json'],text=True)\nd=json.loads(raw[raw.index('{'):])\ndesign=d['design']\n# find migration version\nfor m in re.finditer(r'(?i)migration v\\d|v\\d \\(additive|_migrate_v\\d|## Data Model.*?(?=## Files)', design):\n    pass\nm=re.search(r'## Data Model.*?(?=## Files to Create)', design, re.S)\nprint(m.group(0)[:4500] if m else 'no data model')\nprint('---')\n# files section\nm=re.search(r'## Files to Create.*?(?=## Sequencing)', design, re.S)\nprint(m.group(0)[:2500] if m else 'no files')\nprint('---TECH---')\nm=re.search(r'## Technical Approach.*?(?=## Interface)', design, re.S)\nprint(m.group(0)[:3000] if m else 'n/a')\nPY\n# Check MARATHON-12 for migration version too\npython3 <<'PY'\nimport json,subprocess,re\nfor tid in ['MARATHON-12','MARATHON-10','MARATHON-11']:\n    raw=subprocess.check_output(['loom','data','show',tid,'--output','json'],text=True)\n    d=json.loads(raw[raw.index('{'):])\n    design=d.get('design') or ''\n    notes=d.get('notes') or ''\n    hits=re.findall(r'(?i)migration v\\d|_migrate_v\\d|v\\d \\(', design+notes)\n    print(tid, 'status', d['status'], 'hits', hits[:20], 'has_design', bool(design))\nPY\ncat /work/ws/worktrees/app/app-architect-1/loom-prompts/team-architect.md | head -100\nrg -n -i \"dm|dms\" /work/ws/worktrees/app/app-architect-1/loom-prompts/","workingDirectory":"","exitCode":1,"signal":"","stdout":"## Data Model\n\n### Existing (v3 — unchanged DDL)\n\n`messages(id, channel_id, author_id, body, parent_id, created_at, edited_at, deleted_at)` — already created in MARATHON-7 v3 + `idx_messages_channel_id`.\n\n### Migration v4 (additive, backward compatible)\n\n#### Alter `messages` (optional column)\n\n| Column | Type | Null | Notes |\n|--------|------|------|-------|\n| `blocks_json` | TEXT | NULL | JSON array text; omit from `MessageObj` |\n\nIf implementer prefers zero ALTER, store blocks in a side table — **prefer single nullable column** for simplicity.\n\n#### Indexes on `messages`\n\n- `idx_messages_channel_created` on `(channel_id, created_at DESC, id DESC)` for history.\n- `idx_messages_parent_created` on `(parent_id, created_at ASC, id ASC)` for replies.\n\n#### `message_reactions`\n\n| Column | Type | Null | Notes |\n|--------|------|------|-------|\n| `message_id` | TEXT | NOT NULL | FK → messages ON DELETE CASCADE |\n| `user_id` | TEXT | NOT NULL | FK → users |\n| `emoji` | TEXT | NOT NULL | |\n| `created_at` | TEXT | NOT NULL | |\n| PRIMARY KEY | `(message_id, user_id, emoji)` | | Enforces idempotent POST |\n\nIndex: `(message_id, created_at ASC)` for aggregate ordering.\n\n#### `message_files` (forward seam for MARATHON-10)\n\n| Column | Type | Null | Notes |\n|--------|------|------|-------|\n| `message_id` | TEXT | NOT NULL | FK → messages ON DELETE CASCADE |\n| `file_id` | TEXT | NOT NULL | No FK until M10 `files` exists **or** FK added in M10 |\n| `created_at` | TEXT | NOT NULL | |\n| PRIMARY KEY | `(message_id, file_id)` | | |\n| UNIQUE | `(file_id)` | | A file attaches to at most one message |\n\n**M8 behavior:** if `files` table absent, refuse non-empty `file_ids` with 400 and leave `message_files` unused. M10 creates `files` and wires validation (uploader_id == caller, not already attached).\n\n### Schema change summary\n\n| Change | Compatible? |\n|--------|-------------|\n| v4 new tables + indexes + optional column | **Yes** — additive |\n| v3 messages columns | **Unchanged** |\n\n### Invariants\n\n1. Soft-deleted messages have `deleted_at` non-null; excluded from history/reply lists.\n2. `reply_count` never includes deleted children.\n3. At most one reaction row per `(message_id, user_id, emoji)`.\n4. `parent_id` either NULL or points to top-level message in same channel.\n5. Pin routes continue to work; `get_message` / `row_to_message_obj` enrich reactions/mentions/files/reply_count for pin embeds too.\n6. REST `MessageObj` never carries `seq`.\n\n\n---\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\n\n### Create\n\n| File | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| `tests/test_messages.py` | Contract tests: post/list/pagination/edit/delete/replies/reactions/authz/423/409/empty body/parent rules/idempotent delete & reactions; cluster read-after-write on second node |\n\n### Modify\n\n| File | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| `server/db.py` | `_migrate_v4` / `_reverse_v4`; register in `MIGRATIONS` / `REVERSES` |\n| `server/messages/store.py` | Create/list/get with enrichment; soft delete; reply list; reaction insert/delete/aggregate; mention resolve helper; `row_to_message_obj` fills real `reply_count`/`reactions`/`mentions`/`files` (files empty until M10); keep `MESSAGE_OBJ_KEYS` unchanged |\n| `server/messages/routes.py` | Add PATCH, DELETE, GET replies, POST/DELETE reactions; keep pin routes; factor `_require_pin_access` into shared `_require_message_channel_access(read|write)` used by pins + CRUD + reactions |\n| `server/channels/routes.py` | Add `POST`/`GET /{channel_id}/messages` using messages store + same authz patterns as pins |\n| `server/events.py` | No-op `publish_message_created/updated/deleted`, `publish_reaction_changed` with docstring payloads for M13 (`type`, `channel_id`, message/reactions fields — **seq assigned by M13**) |\n| `server/messages/__init__.py` | Docstring: CRUD owned here |\n| `tests/test_pins.py` | Only if enrichment changes assertions (prefer keep using `MESSAGE_OBJ_KEYS`; update empty `reactions` expectations only if pins now return aggregates — still `[]` without reaction rows) |\n| `tests/test_channels.py` | Optionally assert v4 migration present; do not break v3 assertions |\n\n### Do not touch\n\n| File | Reason |\n|------|--------|\n| `static/**` | MARATHON-17 |\n| `server/auth/**`, `server/users/**` | Consume only |\n| File upload routes | MARATHON-10 |\n| Search / slash / read_state | MARATHON-11 |\n| WS / Redis fan-out | MARATHON-13/14 |\n| DM create | MARATHON-9 |\n\n\n---TECH---\n## Technical Approach\n\n### Decision\n\n| Concern | Choice |\n|--------|--------|\n| Persistence | Shared SQLite SoT; **migration version 4** additive: `message_reactions`, optional `message_files`, optional `messages.blocks_json`; **do not** drop/recreate v3 `messages` |\n| Route layout | Channel-scoped on `server/channels/routes.py`: `POST`/`GET /api/channels/{id}/messages`. Message-scoped on existing `server/messages/routes.py`: `PATCH`/`DELETE /{id}`, `GET /{parent_id}/replies`, `POST`/`DELETE .../reactions`, keep existing pin routes |\n| Auth | Reuse `get_current_user`; all endpoints require Bearer |\n| `MessageObj` | **Same key set as MARATHON-7** (`MESSAGE_OBJ_KEYS`); enrich `reply_count`, `files`, `reactions`, `mentions` for real data; **no `seq`** on REST objects (product: seq lives on WS frames — MARATHON-13) |\n| Soft delete | Set `deleted_at`; **exclude** deleted rows from channel history and reply lists; DELETE idempotent; PATCH / react on deleted → **409** `conflict` |\n| Archived writes | Reuse `server.channels.store.assert_channel_writable` → **423** `archived` for POST message, PATCH, DELETE (mutating), POST/DELETE reaction (same as pin) |\n| Membership for writes | Same as pin mutate: caller must be **channel member**; workspace owner without membership → **403** |\n| Membership for reads | Same as GET pins: public → workspace member else 404; private/DM → channel member else 403 (non-member workspace) / 404 (stranger) |\n| Threads | Single level only: `parent_id` must reference a **top-level** (`parent_id IS NULL`), same-channel, non-deleted message |\n| Reactions | Table unique on `(message_id, user_id, emoji)`; aggregate to `[{emoji, count, user_ids}]`; POST/DELETE idempotent |\n| `file_ids` / `blocks` | Accept on POST for product shape; `blocks` persisted as JSON text (not exposed on `MessageObj` keys); `file_ids` validated against files when present — **non-empty `file_ids` → 400** until MARATHON-10 provides attachable `files` rows (seam documented) |\n| Mentions (this task) | Resolve `@username` only (word-boundary, no mid-word, no self, dedupe, must be workspace member); `@channel`/`@here`/`@group-handle` → MARATHON-11 |\n| Slash commands | **Not** interpreted here; body stored literally (including `/me`, `/shrug`, …). MARATHON-11 intercepts |\n| Events | Add no-op hooks in `server/events.py` (`publish_message_created` / `updated` / `deleted` / `reaction_changed`) for MARATHON-13; call after successful commit; must not raise |\n| Timestamps | `utc_now_z()` (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`) |\n| Ids | UUID4 strings |\n\n### Why\n\n- Product contract (epic planning) is explicit on paths, envelopes (`201 {message}`, `{replies}`, `{reactions}`, `{deleted:true}`), reaction shape, and delete/edit conflict semantics; inventing alternates breaks graders and MARATHON-17.\n- Extending v3 (not replacing) keeps pin FKs and MARATHON-7 tests stable; `MESSAGE_OBJ_KEYS` remains the single key source (pin tests import it).\n- Aligning write authz with pin membership + `ass\nMARATHON-12 status review hits ['Migration v3', '_migrate_v4', 'Migration v4', 'Migration v4'] has_design True\nMARATHON-10 status open hits [] has_design False\nMARATHON-11 status open hits [] has_design False\n<!-- ROLE-MARKER: team-architect -->\n## WORKFLOW: Architecture Task (Design Only - No Implementation)\n\nYou are a disciplined software architect. Your job is to CREATE DESIGNS, not implement them.\nFollow this workflow EXACTLY for ONE task.\n\n**Your agent name is: {{ .AgentName }}** (Loom actor is set automatically)\n**You are working as the {{ .Role }} agent role.**\n{{ .WorkspaceBlock }}{{ .EpicScope }}{{ .SafetyBlock }}{{ .CheckpointBlock }}\n### Your Domain\n\n{{if eq .Role \"agent-architect\"}}Your domain is agent systems: the prompt, the tool surface, and the control loop ARE the design artifacts. Every design must state what the agent is told, which tools it may call, how the loop terminates, what happens on a tool error or a refusal, and how a behavior change will be observed (a log line, a transcript artifact, or an eval case).{{else if eq .Role \"api-architect\"}}Your domain is the API contract and the data model. Every design must state the endpoints or interfaces with their exact request/response shapes, the persisted schema with types and nullability, the migration path from the current schema, and the compatibility story for existing callers.{{else}}Your domain is a full-stack application: the frontend/backend seam IS the design artifact. Every design must state which side owns which behavior, the exact shape of the data crossing the seam, and what each side does when the other side fails or is slow.{{end}}\n\n### Your Lane\n\n- You produce designs. You do NOT write application code, tests, or migrations — not \"just a small one\", not \"to prove it works\".\n- You claim ONLY tasks carrying the `architect` label. Unlabeled work belongs to the implementer agent roles; do not go looking for it.\n- Your output is a design saved on the task, plus notes. Nothing else you write survives, so put every decision in the design.\n- If a task is labeled `architect` but is genuinely trivial (a typo, a one-line config change), say so in the notes, remove nothing, and hand it back for review rather than inventing architecture for it.\n- Never write code and never run the app.\n\n### Step 0: Sync with the Integrated Head\n\n- Run `git merge --no-edit main` before grounding yourself. Your worktree is never synced by the supervisor, and every design must be based on the harness-integrated code.\n- Resolve merge conflicts before continuing. This sync is for reading the current code only: never write implementation code and never run the app.\n\n### Step 1: Select ONE Architecture Task\n{{if .TaskID}}\nA task is already claimed for you: **{{ .TaskID }}**. Work on that one and no other.\n\n{{ .TaskDetail }}\n{{else}}\n- Run this command to find tasks waiting on design:\n  loom data ready --limit 200 --output json | jq -r '.[] | select(.status == \"open\") | select((.issue_type == \"epic\") | not) | select((.labels // []) | index(\"architect\")) | \"\\(.id) [\\(.priority)] \\(.title)\"'\n- If jq fails, fallback: run 'loom data ready --limit 200', open the candidates with 'loom data show <id>', and keep only the ones carrying the `architect` label\n- SKIP any task already 'in_progress' by checking 'loom data list --status in_progress'\n- IGNORE existing assignees - if status is 'open', the task is available to claim\n- Pick the HIGHEST PRIORITY task (P0 > P1 > P2 > P3 > P4)\n- Run 'loom data show <id>' to understand the task requirements\n- Run 'loom data claim <id>' to claim it (atomic - prevents race conditions)\n- If claim fails with 'already claimed by X', pick the next highest priority task\n- Run 'loom claim <id>' to register the task with the agent monitor\n- REMEMBER this task ID\n- If NO `architect`-labeled task is available: run 'loom complete' and EXIT immediately. Do NOT claim unlabeled work.\n{{end}}\n### Step 1.5: Check if This is a Revision\n\nRun 'loom data show <id> --output json' and check the labels field.\n\n**If the task has a 'needs-revision' label:** a previous design was rejected. Read the comments and notes for the feedback, read the existing design for context, and make sure your new design addresses the specific objection.\n\n**If not:** this is a new design.\n\n### Step 2: Ground Yourself Before Designing\n\nDo NOT skip any sub-step. A design written without this context contradicts its siblings and gets rejected.\n\n#### 2a. Read the Epic\n- Determine the parent epic from the task ID (e.g. `proj-abc.5` -> parent is `proj-abc`) or from the parent field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'\n- Run 'loom data show <epic-id>' and read the title, description, and notes\n- Epic notes carry architectural decisions, naming conventions, and scope boundaries. They are **authoritative** — your design conforms to them or explicitly argues why not.\n\n#### 2b. Read Sibling Designs\n- Run: loom data list --parent <epic-id> --output json | jq -r '.[] | select((.has_design == true) or ((.design_artifact_id // \"\") != \"\") or ((.design // \"\") != \"\")) | \"\\(.id) \\(.title)\"'\n- Read each sibling design and extract the conventions already fixed: names, identity and key formats, interface contracts, error semantics\n- Your design MUST stay consistent with them. To diverge, say so in the Technical Approach and justify it.\n\n#### 2c. Read the Actual Code\n- Find the modules this change touches and read them — not just their names\n- Identify existing patterns to follow, and the seams the change will cross\n- Note dependencies, blockers, and anything already half-built in this direction\n\n#### 2d. Scan the Neighborhood (MANDATORY)\nFor every file you expect to be modified: list its directory, skim the sibling files, and grep the repository for the pattern you are changing. Account for every hit — either it is in scope, or it goes in the \"Out of Scope\" section with the reason.\n\n### Step 3: Write the Design\n{{- if eq .DesignFormat \"html\"}}\n\n**Design format: HTML.** Author the design as semantic HTML instead of Markdown: `<h2>` for headings, `<p>` for prose, `<ul>`/`<li>` for lists, `<pre><code>` for code and commands. Produce the same sections listed below. Do NOT include an `<html>`, `<head>`, or `<body>` wrapper, and do NOT use inline styles or scripts. When a diagram would materially clarify a seam or a flow, embed a self-contained inline `<svg>` using `<rect>`, `<line>`, `<path>`, `<circle>` and `<text>` with presentation attributes — no `<script>`, no external images, no mermaid.\n{{- end}}\n\nCover every section:\n\n#### 3a. Summary\nOne paragraph: what this task accomplishes and which problem it solves.\n\n#### 3b. Technical Approach\nThe architecture decision, the alternatives considered, and why this one. Name the trade-off you accepted.\n\n#### 3c. Interface Contracts\n{{if eq .Role \"agent-architect\"}}The prompt's job statement, the exact tool list with argument shapes, the loop's termination conditions, and the retry/refusal behavior.{{else if eq .Role \"api-architect\"}}Every endpoint or interface: method, path or signature, request shape, response shape, status codes, and error bodies. Field types and nullability are part of the contract, not a detail.{{else}}Every contract crossing the frontend/backend seam: the payload shape both sides agree on, who validates what, and what the client renders while the server is slow or failing.{{end}}\nState them precisely enough that two different implementer agent roles, working in parallel and never talking to each other, produce code that fits together.\n\n#### 3d. Data Model\n{{if eq .Role \"agent-architect\"}}The state the loop carries between turns, what is persisted versus recomputed, and what a resumed run must reconstruct.{{else}}Entities, fields, types, nullability, and relationships. Call out every schema change and whether it is backward compatible.{{end}}\n\n#### 3e. Files to Create / Files to Modify\nList them at file level with the change each one needs. This list is what makes the work splittable — an implementer should not have to guess where the code goes.\n\n#### 3f. Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\n","stderr":"time=2026-08-23T00:25:52.736Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:44829\ntime=2026-08-23T00:25:52.769Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:44829\ntime=2026-08-23T00:25:52.782Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:44829\ntime=2026-08-23T00:25:52.827Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:44829\n","executionTime":222,"interleavedOutput":"time=2026-08-23T00:25:52.736Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:44829\n## Data Model\n\n### Existing (v3 — unchanged DDL)\n\n`messages(id, channel_id, author_id, body, parent_id, created_at, edited_at, deleted_at)` — already created in MARATHON-7 v3 + `idx_messages_channel_id`.\n\n### Migration v4 (additive, backward compatible)\n\n#### Alter `messages` (optional column)\n\n| Column | Type | Null | Notes |\n|--------|------|------|-------|\n| `blocks_json` | TEXT | NULL | JSON array text; omit from `MessageObj` |\n\nIf implementer prefers zero ALTER, store blocks in a side table — **prefer single nullable column** for simplicity.\n\n#### Indexes on `messages`\n\n- `idx_messages_channel_created` on `(channel_id, created_at DESC, id DESC)` for history.\n- `idx_messages_parent_created` on `(parent_id, created_at ASC, id ASC)` for replies.\n\n#### `message_reactions`\n\n| Column | Type | Null | Notes |\n|--------|------|------|-------|\n| `message_id` | TEXT | NOT NULL | FK → messages ON DELETE CASCADE |\n| `user_id` | TEXT | NOT NULL | FK → users |\n| `emoji` | TEXT | NOT NULL | |\n| `created_at` | TEXT | NOT NULL | |\n| PRIMARY KEY | `(message_id, user_id, emoji)` | | Enforces idempotent POST |\n\nIndex: `(message_id, created_at ASC)` for aggregate ordering.\n\n#### `message_files` (forward seam for MARATHON-10)\n\n| Column | Type | Null | Notes |\n|--------|------|------|-------|\n| `message_id` | TEXT | NOT NULL | FK → messages ON DELETE CASCADE |\n| `file_id` | TEXT | NOT NULL | No FK until M10 `files` exists **or** FK added in M10 |\n| `created_at` | TEXT | NOT NULL | |\n| PRIMARY KEY | `(message_id, file_id)` | | |\n| UNIQUE | `(file_id)` | | A file attaches to at most one message |\n\n**M8 behavior:** if `files` table absent, refuse non-empty `file_ids` with 400 and leave `message_files` unused. M10 creates `files` and wires validation (uploader_id == caller, not already attached).\n\n### Schema change summary\n\n| Change | Compatible? |\n|--------|-------------|\n| v4 new tables + indexes + optional column | **Yes** — additive |\n| v3 messages columns | **Unchanged** |\n\n### Invariants\n\n1. Soft-deleted messages have `deleted_at` non-null; excluded from history/reply lists.\n2. `reply_count` never includes deleted children.\n3. At most one reaction row per `(message_id, user_id, emoji)`.\n4. `parent_id` either NULL or points to top-level message in same channel.\n5. Pin routes continue to work; `get_message` / `row_to_message_obj` enrich reactions/mentions/files/reply_count for pin embeds too.\n6. REST `MessageObj` never carries `seq`.\n\n\n---\n## Files to Create / Files to Modify\n\n### Create\n\n| File | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| `tests/test_messages.py` | Contract tests: post/list/pagination/edit/delete/replies/reactions/authz/423/409/empty body/parent rules/idempotent delete & reactions; cluster read-after-write on second node |\n\n### Modify\n\n| File | Change |\n|------|--------|\n| `server/db.py` | `_migrate_v4` / `_reverse_v4`; register in `MIGRATIONS` / `REVERSES` |\n| `server/messages/store.py` | Create/list/get with enrichment; soft delete; reply list; reaction insert/delete/aggregate; mention resolve helper; `row_to_message_obj` fills real `reply_count`/`reactions`/`mentions`/`files` (files empty until M10); keep `MESSAGE_OBJ_KEYS` unchanged |\n| `server/messages/routes.py` | Add PATCH, DELETE, GET replies, POST/DELETE reactions; keep pin routes; factor `_require_pin_access` into shared `_require_message_channel_access(read|write)` used by pins + CRUD + reactions |\n| `server/channels/routes.py` | Add `POST`/`GET /{channel_id}/messages` using messages store + same authz patterns as pins |\n| `server/events.py` | No-op `publish_message_created/updated/deleted`, `publish_reaction_changed` with docstring payloads for M13 (`type`, `channel_id`, message/reactions fields — **seq assigned by M13**) |\n| `server/messages/__init__.py` | Docstring: CRUD owned here |\n| `tests/test_pins.py` | Only if enrichment changes assertions (prefer keep using `MESSAGE_OBJ_KEYS`; update empty `reactions` expectations only if pins now return aggregates — still `[]` without reaction rows) |\n| `tests/test_channels.py` | Optionally assert v4 migration present; do not break v3 assertions |\n\n### Do not touch\n\n| File | Reason |\n|------|--------|\n| `static/**` | MARATHON-17 |\n| `server/auth/**`, `server/users/**` | Consume only |\n| File upload routes | MARATHON-10 |\n| Search / slash / read_state | MARATHON-11 |\n| WS / Redis fan-out | MARATHON-13/14 |\n| DM create | MARATHON-9 |\n\n\n---TECH---\n## Technical Approach\n\n### Decision\n\n| Concern | Choice |\n|--------|--------|\n| Persistence | Shared SQLite SoT; **migration version 4** additive: `message_reactions`, optional `message_files`, optional `messages.blocks_json`; **do not** drop/recreate v3 `messages` |\n| Route layout | Channel-scoped on `server/channels/routes.py`: `POST`/`GET /api/channels/{id}/messages`. Message-scoped on existing `server/messages/routes.py`: `PATCH`/`DELETE /{id}`, `GET /{parent_id}/replies`, `POST`/`DELETE .../reactions`, keep existing pin routes |\n| Auth | Reuse `get_current_user`; all endpoints require Bearer |\n| `MessageObj` | **Same key set as MARATHON-7** (`MESSAGE_OBJ_KEYS`); enrich `reply_count`, `files`, `reactions`, `mentions` for real data; **no `seq`** on REST objects (product: seq lives on WS frames — MARATHON-13) |\n| Soft delete | Set `deleted_at`; **exclude** deleted rows from channel history and reply lists; DELETE idempotent; PATCH / react on deleted → **409** `conflict` |\n| Archived writes | Reuse `server.channels.store.assert_channel_writable` → **423** `archived` for POST message, PATCH, DELETE (mutating), POST/DELETE reaction (same as pin) |\n| Membership for writes | Same as pin mutate: caller must be **channel member**; workspace owner without membership → **403** |\n| Membership for reads | Same as GET pins: public → workspace member else 404; private/DM → channel member else 403 (non-member workspace) / 404 (stranger) |\n| Threads | Single level only: `parent_id` must reference a **top-level** (`parent_id IS NULL`), same-channel, non-deleted message |\n| Reactions | Table unique on `(message_id, user_id, emoji)`; aggregate to `[{emoji, count, user_ids}]`; POST/DELETE idempotent |\n| `file_ids` / `blocks` | Accept on POST for product shape; `blocks` persisted as JSON text (not exposed on `MessageObj` keys); `file_ids` validated against files when present — **non-empty `file_ids` → 400** until MARATHON-10 provides attachable `files` rows (seam documented) |\n| Mentions (this task) | Resolve `@username` only (word-boundary, no mid-word, no self, dedupe, must be workspace member); `@channel`/`@here`/`@group-handle` → MARATHON-11 |\n| Slash commands | **Not** interpreted here; body stored literally (including `/me`, `/shrug`, …). MARATHON-11 intercepts |\n| Events | Add no-op hooks in `server/events.py` (`publish_message_created` / `updated` / `deleted` / `reaction_changed`) for MARATHON-13; call after successful commit; must not raise |\n| Timestamps | `utc_now_z()` (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`) |\n| Ids | UUID4 strings |\n\n### Why\n\n- Product contract (epic planning) is explicit on paths, envelopes (`201 {message}`, `{replies}`, `{reactions}`, `{deleted:true}`), reaction shape, and delete/edit conflict semantics; inventing alternates breaks graders and MARATHON-17.\n- Extending v3 (not replacing) keeps pin FKs and MARATHON-7 tests stable; `MESSAGE_OBJ_KEYS` remains the single key source (pin tests import it).\n- Aligning write authz with pin membership + `ass\ntime=2026-08-23T00:25:52.769Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:44829\ntime=2026-08-23T00:25:52.782Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:44829\ntime=2026-08-23T00:25:52.827Z level=INFO msg=\"opened existing embedded fleet-db client\" url=http://127.0.0.1:44829\nMARATHON-12 status review hits ['Migration v3', '_migrate_v4', 'Migration v4', 'Migration v4'] has_design True\nMARATHON-10 status open hits [] has_design False\nMARATHON-11 status open hits [] has_design False\n<!-- ROLE-MARKER: team-architect -->\n## WORKFLOW: Architecture Task (Design Only - No Implementation)\n\nYou are a disciplined software architect. Your job is to CREATE DESIGNS, not implement them.\nFollow this workflow EXACTLY for ONE task.\n\n**Your agent name is: {{ .AgentName }}** (Loom actor is set automatically)\n**You are working as the {{ .Role }} agent role.**\n{{ .WorkspaceBlock }}{{ .EpicScope }}{{ .SafetyBlock }}{{ .CheckpointBlock }}\n### Your Domain\n\n{{if eq .Role \"agent-architect\"}}Your domain is agent systems: the prompt, the tool surface, and the control loop ARE the design artifacts. Every design must state what the agent is told, which tools it may call, how the loop terminates, what happens on a tool error or a refusal, and how a behavior change will be observed (a log line, a transcript artifact, or an eval case).{{else if eq .Role \"api-architect\"}}Your domain is the API contract and the data model. Every design must state the endpoints or interfaces with their exact request/response shapes, the persisted schema with types and nullability, the migration path from the current schema, and the compatibility story for existing callers.{{else}}Your domain is a full-stack application: the frontend/backend seam IS the design artifact. Every design must state which side owns which behavior, the exact shape of the data crossing the seam, and what each side does when the other side fails or is slow.{{end}}\n\n### Your Lane\n\n- You produce designs. You do NOT write application code, tests, or migrations — not \"just a small one\", not \"to prove it works\".\n- You claim ONLY tasks carrying the `architect` label. Unlabeled work belongs to the implementer agent roles; do not go looking for it.\n- Your output is a design saved on the task, plus notes. Nothing else you write survives, so put every decision in the design.\n- If a task is labeled `architect` but is genuinely trivial (a typo, a one-line config change), say so in the notes, remove nothing, and hand it back for review rather than inventing architecture for it.\n- Never write code and never run the app.\n\n### Step 0: Sync with the Integrated Head\n\n- Run `git merge --no-edit main` before grounding yourself. Your worktree is never synced by the supervisor, and every design must be based on the harness-integrated code.\n- Resolve merge conflicts before continuing. This sync is for reading the current code only: never write implementation code and never run the app.\n\n### Step 1: Select ONE Architecture Task\n{{if .TaskID}}\nA task is already claimed for you: **{{ .TaskID }}**. Work on that one and no other.\n\n{{ .TaskDetail }}\n{{else}}\n- Run this command to find tasks waiting on design:\n  loom data ready --limit 200 --output json | jq -r '.[] | select(.status == \"open\") | select((.issue_type == \"epic\") | not) | select((.labels // []) | index(\"architect\")) | \"\\(.id) [\\(.priority)] \\(.title)\"'\n- If jq fails, fallback: run 'loom data ready --limit 200', open the candidates with 'loom data show <id>', and keep only the ones carrying the `architect` label\n- SKIP any task already 'in_progress' by checking 'loom data list --status in_progress'\n- IGNORE existing assignees - if status is 'open', the task is available to claim\n- Pick the HIGHEST PRIORITY task (P0 > P1 > P2 > P3 > P4)\n- Run 'loom data show <id>' to understand the task requirements\n- Run 'loom data claim <id>' to claim it (atomic - prevents race conditions)\n- If claim fails with 'already claimed by X', pick the next highest priority task\n- Run 'loom claim <id>' to register the task with the agent monitor\n- REMEMBER this task ID\n- If NO `architect`-labeled task is available: run 'loom complete' and EXIT immediately. Do NOT claim unlabeled work.\n{{end}}\n### Step 1.5: Check if This is a Revision\n\nRun 'loom data show <id> --output json' and check the labels field.\n\n**If the task has a 'needs-revision' label:** a previous design was rejected. Read the comments and notes for the feedback, read the existing design for context, and make sure your new design addresses the specific objection.\n\n**If not:** this is a new design.\n\n### Step 2: Ground Yourself Before Designing\n\nDo NOT skip any sub-step. A design written without this context contradicts its siblings and gets rejected.\n\n#### 2a. Read the Epic\n- Determine the parent epic from the task ID (e.g. `proj-abc.5` -> parent is `proj-abc`) or from the parent field of 'loom data show <id> --output json'\n- Run 'loom data show <epic-id>' and read the title, description, and notes\n- Epic notes carry architectural decisions, naming conventions, and scope boundaries. They are **authoritative** — your design conforms to them or explicitly argues why not.\n\n#### 2b. Read Sibling Designs\n- Run: loom data list --parent <epic-id> --output json | jq -r '.[] | select((.has_design == true) or ((.design_artifact_id // \"\") != \"\") or ((.design // \"\") != \"\")) | \"\\(.id) \\(.title)\"'\n- Read each sibling design and extract the conventions already fixed: names, identity and key formats, interface contracts, error semantics\n- Your design MUST stay consistent with them. To diverge, say so in the Technical Approach and justify it.\n\n#### 2c. Read the Actual Code\n- Find the modules this change touches and read them — not just their names\n- Identify existing patterns to follow, and the seams the change will cross\n- Note dependencies, blockers, and anything already half-built in this direction\n\n#### 2d. Scan the Neighborhood (MANDATORY)\nFor every file you expect to be modified: list its directory, skim the sibling files, and grep the repository for the pattern you are changing. Account for every hit — either it is in scope, or it goes in the \"Out of Scope\" section with the reason.\n\n### Step 3: Write the Design\n{{- if eq .DesignFormat \"html\"}}\n\n**Design format: HTML.** Author the design as semantic HTML instead of Markdown: `<h2>` for headings, `<p>` for prose, `<ul>`/`<li>` for lists, `<pre><code>` for code and commands. Produce the same sections listed below. Do NOT include an `<html>`, `<head>`, or `<body>` wrapper, and do NOT use inline styles or scripts. When a diagram would materially clarify a seam or a flow, embed a self-contained inline `<svg>` using `<rect>`, `<line>`, `<path>`, `<circle>` and `<text>` with presentation attributes — no `<script>`, no external images, no mermaid.\n{{- end}}\n\nCover every section:\n\n#### 3a. Summary\nOne paragraph: what this task accomplishes and which problem it solves.\n\n#### 3b. Technical Approach\nThe architecture decision, the alternatives considered, and why this one. Name the trade-off you accepted.\n\n#### 3c. Interface Contracts\n{{if eq .Role \"agent-architect\"}}The prompt's job statement, the exact tool list with argument shapes, the loop's termination conditions, and the retry/refusal behavior.{{else if eq .Role \"api-architect\"}}Every endpoint or interface: method, path or signature, request shape, response shape, status codes, and error bodies. Field types and nullability are part of the contract, not a detail.{{else}}Every contract crossing the frontend/backend seam: the payload shape both sides agree on, who validates what, and what the client renders while the server is slow or failing.{{end}}\nState them precisely enough that two different implementer agent roles, working in parallel and never talking to each other, produce code that fits together.\n\n#### 3d. Data Model\n{{if eq .Role \"agent-architect\"}}The state the loop carries between turns, what is persisted versus recomputed, and what a resumed run must reconstruct.{{else}}Entities, fields, types, nullability, and relationships. Call out every schema change and whether it is backward compatible.{{end}}\n\n#### 3e. Files to Create / Files to Modify\nList them at file level with the change each one needs. This list is what makes the work splittable — an implementer should not have to guess where the code goes.\n\n#### 3f. Sequencing and Cross-Task Seams\n","aborted":false,"localExecutionTimeMs":158},"isBackground":false},"description":"Check migration version conflicts"},"hookAdditionalContexts":[],"toolCallId":"call-949fce16-96ba-490b-9b06-62888c436630-24\nfc_cffa8d6c-3d10-9a76-8cd0-6822c5b29e33_0","startedAtMs":"1787444752645","completedAtMs":"1787444752867"},"model_call_id":"b6dbef0b-e6fb-4ca9-8c38-d48246858510-7-dqvm","session_id":"3f743427-f194-44bc-92dc-836b7b6940f7","timestamp_ms":1787444752893}
